Exercise 2: Use yt-dlp in a container to download youtube videos
......Dockerfile
FROM ubuntu:24.04

WORKDIR /mydir

RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y curl python3 ffmpeg && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*
# downloads 3 commands at once curl, python3, ffmpeg 
# also running apt-get update && install instead of running separate RUN update and install is standard
#  rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*  - removes the downloaded package index files afterward, since they're not needed once installation is done.

RUN curl -L https://github.com/yt-dlp/yt-dlp/releases/latest/download/yt-dlp -o /usr/local/bin/yt-dlp
#  curl -L https://github.com/yt-dlp/yt-dlp/releases/latest/download/yt-dlp - download link
#  -o /usr/local/bin/yt-dlp - download destination

RUN chmod a+rx /usr/local/bin/yt-dlp #give permissions

ENTRYPOINT ["/usr/local/bin/yt-dlp"] #to be run with : docker run -t image "www.youtube.com"
#ENTRYPOINT is far better suited than CMD for this Dockerfile because yt-dlp is a command-line tool designed to take arguments (YouTube URLs, output formats, download flags).
#when there is no default command, CMD [--help] which will return a prompt to input a link or keyword that works with ENTRYPOINT

CMD ["https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ddyBbmhvDzM"] #Both ENTRYPOINT & CMD can be used together in this context,
if a link isn't specified at docker run, it will run CMD's default. docker run with link specified runs ENTRYPOINT.

.............
* docker build -t wicked -f Dockerfile.yt .
* docker run wicked # runs the CMD Default
Best way to access and download file
Syntax: docker run -v /host/path:/container/path image_name [command]
* docker run -v --rm "$(pwd):/docking" brymo https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9D1_TnXvtWY #simpler, drops directly in directory /docking 
* docker run --rm -v "$(pwd)/downloads:/downloads" wasted -o "/downloads/%(title)s.%(ext)s" https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2D2Z-eqK0YM
# -v "$(pwd)/downloads:/downloads"- Mount your local downloads folder to /downloads inside the container
# wicked - The image name # --rm - To clean up the container after it finishes:
# -o "/downloads/%(title)s.%(ext)s"	 - The -o option passed to yt-dlp (where to save)
# https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2D2Z-eqK0YM	- The video URL to download

